  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How can a small pump lift something so heavy?",
      "a": "Pressure in the oil is the same everywhere. The ram has a much larger area than the pump piston, so the same pressure gives it a much larger force, though it moves a shorter distance.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a garage lift raise a whole car?",
        "What is Pascal’s principle?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is oil used in hydraulics?",
      "a": "Oil, like other liquids, hardly squashes, so a push at one end arrives at the other straight away. Oil also lubricates the parts and does not rust them like water would.",
      "asks": [
        "Why not use air or water in a car lift?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a car lift come back down?",
      "a": "Opening the lowering valve lets oil flow out of the ram back into the reservoir. The weight of the car pushes the oil out, so it sinks slowly and smoothly.",
      "asks": [
        "What lowers a hydraulic lift?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Is there a catch to getting a bigger force?",
      "a": "Yes. Energy is not created, so the small piston has to move much farther than the ram rises. You trade distance for force, like a lever.",
      "asks": [
        "Do hydraulics give you free energy?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Where else are hydraulics used?",
      "a": "In car brakes, diggers, dentist chairs, aircraft controls and the jacks used to change a tyre.",
      "asks": [
        "What other machines use hydraulics?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Pascal’s principle: pressure applied to an enclosed liquid is passed on equally to every part of it.",
    "A ram with 50 times the area of the pump piston gives 50 times the force, but moves only one fiftieth as far."
  ],
